## Procedure
1. Coat three wells of a six-well plate with Geltrex as described in Step 1B(i). Dissociate plated hiPSCs as described in Steps 1A(ii) and 1A(iii). Resuspend $6\times10^{5}$ cells in $6~\mathsf{m l}$ of Essential 8 medium with $10\upmu\mathsf{M}$ ROCK inhibitor and split over three $15-\mathsf{m l}$ tubes. Quickly thaw one vial of the CAG-GFP/RFP lentivirus and test three different volumes by adding 2, 5 and $10~\upmu\upiota$ to determine the optimal trans­ duction efficiency of the virus. Mix well and plate the infected hiPSCs in the wells. Incubate in a $5\%$ ${\mathsf{C}}0_{2}$ incubator overnight.
$\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL STEP Split the remaining virus into $2-\upmu\updownarrow$ aliquots and store at $-80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to avoid freeze–thaw cycles, which will decrease the transduction efficiency of the virus. The virus can be maintained without a substantial change in transduction efficiency for up to year. Use bleach to clean all equipment that has come into contact with the virus.   
2. The next morning, change the medium to Essential 8 to avoid cell toxicity. Then over the next days, change the medium to Essential 8 daily and determine the transduction efficiency using a fluorescence microscope to check GFP/RFP expression. When the cells reach $60{-}70\%$ confluence (after ${\sim}2\ –3\ d$ ), dissociate the cells in the well with the highest GFP/RFP expression and replate ${\sim}5\times10^{4}$ cells in two Geltrex-coated sterile $10\mathrm{-cm}$ Petri dishes (in Essential 8 with $10~\upmu\upmu$ ROCK inhibitor).   
3. Continue to change Essential 8 medium daily, and then $2-3{\mathsf{d}}$ later single cells will form colonies. Pick single GFP/RFP-only colonies using a $20\mathrm{-}\upmu\updownarrow$ pipette under a fluorescence microscope in a cell culture hood. Transfer the colonies to Geltrex-coated wells of a 24-well plate containing Essential 8, $10~\upmu\upmu$ ROCK inhibitor and penicillin–streptomycin.   
4. Grow the cells for $3-4=1$ and then replate only the colonies in which all cells have GFP/RFP expression. Replate each colony to a new ell of six-well plate and then subsequently expand the cells by growing them in a T25 flask.   
5. When GFP-tagged hiPSCs reach $70\mathrm{-}80\%$ confluence (after ${\sim}3\mathrm{~d~}$ ), split them into four cryovials with $10\%$ (vol/vol) DMSO in MEF medium and freeze them at $80~\mathsf{C}$ for $2\mathsf{d}$ and then store them in liquid nitrogen.
 CRITICAL STEP To improve the survival of the colonies picked in step 3, keep the cells in the same Essential 8 medium with ROCK inhibitor for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ .
PAUSE POINTCells can be stored in liquid nitrogen for at least 10 years.
6. To thaw the cells, prepare $4~\mathsf{m l}$ of MEF medium prewarmed to $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in a $15-\mathsf{m l}$ tube. Quickly thaw the frozen cells by moving the cry­ ovial from liquid nitrogen directly to a $37^{\circ}\complement$ water bath, and before the cells are completely thawed, add $1~{\mathfrak{m}}!$ of the prewarmed MEF medium directly to the cells in the cryovial. Mix well with the rest of the MEF medium in the $15-\mathsf{m l}$ tube. Centrifuge for $3{\mathrm{~min}}$ at $270g$ at room temperature. Aspirate the supernatant and resuspend the cells in Essential 8 medium with $10~\upmu\upmu$ ROCK inhibitor (Y27632) and replate them in a T25 flask coated with Matrigel as in Step 1B(i).
