Box 2 | Analysis of in vitro-matured PSC-derived cardiomyocytes ● TIMING 1.5–24 h per heart, 30 d after cell culture
1. Various methods can be used to analyze the cells. Follow option A for immunostaining and nucleation analysis; follow option B for a further in vitro cell culture and follow option C for sorting.
## (A) Immunostaining and nucleation analysis
(i) Fix in vitro-plated PSC-CMs directly with $4\%$ (wt/vol) paraformaldehyde in PBS for $30~\mathrm{min}$ .   
(ii) Wash the fixed cells twice for 5 min with PBS at room temperature.   
(iii) Stain for 5 min with DAPI (1:2,000) in PBS, followed by two washings for 5 min with PBS at room temperature. Keep the cells in PBS.
 PAUSE POINT The cells can either be stored overnight at $4^{\circ}\complement$ or imaged immediately.
(iv) Image the cells. Save images and manually count and analyze the number of nuclei per cell.   
(B) Further culture of cells   
(i) Replate the cells by dissociating with TrypLE (as described in Steps 1A(ii) and 1A(iii)) and replating on a gelatin-coated 5-mm round cover glass in either SFD medium for mouse CMs or RPMI plus B27 minus vitamin A with $10\%$ (vol/vol) FBS and $10~\upmu\upmu$ ROCK inhibitor for human $C M s$ to increase cell survival.   
(ii) Transfer the cover glass with plated CMs to the perfusion chamber just before measurement of $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ transients and sarcomere shortening   
(iii) Measure whole-cell $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ transients and sarcomere shortening as described in Step 19A.   
(C) Sorting of cells   
(i) Sort cells as single cells using a cell sorter, or pick single cells directly under a microscope (EVOS) and transfer them to a 96-well plate for cDNA preparation as above.
(xii) Centrifuge sorted Isl1- ${\mathsf{R F P}}^{+}$ CPCs for 3 min at $270g$ at room temperature. Aspirate the supernatant and resuspend the cells in IMDM with Geltrex at a 60:1 ratio to a final concentration of $2\times10^{3}$ cells per ml. Keep CPCs on ice before transplantation.
$\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL STEP To avoid the polymerization of Geltrex, which will increase the viscosity of the cell sample and wil clog the microneedle during cell injection, keep the sample on ice at all times for no more than $4\ h$ .
 PAUSE POINT Cells can be kept on ice for up to 4 h. Cell death increases with time spent on ice; thus, this should be minimized.
(B) Generation of human iPSC-derived cardiac progenitor cells in vitro $0$ TIMING 5–6 d
(i) Generate a constitutively expressing GFP/RFP, hiPSC line (Box 1). Grow hiPSCs on Geltrex-coated T25 flasks using Essential 8 medium in a $5\%$ ${\mathsf{C}}0_{2}$ incubator set at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ until they reach $70\mathrm{-}80\%$ confluence (this usually occurs after 3–4 d), at which point you should proceed to the next step to start cell differentiation. Grow cells in a $5\%$ ${\mathsf{C}}0_{2}$ incubator set at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ throughout the experiment.  CRITICAL STEP Coat each T25 flask with $3~{\mathfrak{m l}}$ of 1:60 Geltrex in DMEM for 30–120 min at room temperature. Determine how many T25 flasks are needed based on the desired cell number. Each T25 flask, when $70\mathrm{-}80\%$ confluent, contains ${\sim}3{\-}4\times10^{6}$ cells.   
(ii) Coat at least two wells of a six-well plate with Geltrex in DMEM (1:60) using $2~\mathsf{m l}$ per well.   
(iii) Rinse the cells from Step B(i) once with $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}\cdot$ and $\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ -free DPBS and then dissociate into single cells by adding 1 ml of TrypLE and incubating at $37^{\circ}\complement$ for $3{\mathrm{~min}}$ .
Box 3 | Immunofluorescence staining of fixed cells for flow cytometry $\bigcirc$ TIM­ ING 2–2.5 h
1. Centrifuge the cell mix for 3 min at $270g$ at room temperature. Aspirate the supernatant and resuspend the cells in $4\%$ (wt/vol) paraformaldehyde in PBS. Incubate for 30 min at room temperature to fix the cells.
2. Centrifuge the cell mix for 3 min at $895g$ at room temperature. Aspirate the supernatant and resuspend the cells in PBS to wash th PFA. Repeat this step once more.
3. Aspirate the supernatant and resuspend the cells in FACS immunostaining solution. Split the sample in two and add mouse anti-Isl antibody (1:500 dilution) to one sample and use the other as a negative control. Incubate for 30 min at room temperature.
4. Wash the cells twice as described in step 2, using FACS immunostaining solution instead of PBS. Aspirate the supernatant and resuspend both cell samples in FACS immunostaining solution with 1:500 donkey anti-mouse IgG $(\mathsf{H}+\mathsf{L})$ secondary antibody, Alexa Fluor 647 conjugate. Incubate for $30~\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature in the dark. Keep the cells in the dark from now on to avoid fluoro­ chrome bleaching.
CRITICAL STEP Use an anti-mouse secondary antibody with high excitation and emission wavelengths to avoid nonspecific eparation of the GFP-tagged CPCs.
(iv) Inactivate the TrypLE by adding 4 ml of MEF medium. Prepare the cells for counting by diluting the resuspended cells at a 1:10 ratio in MEF medium and mixing well with a pipette. Count the cells; we use the Scepter system. To use this, attach the $60\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ Scepter sensor to the Scepter 2.0 handheld automated cell counter and then aspirate the cells. The total cell number equals the number provided by the automated counter multiplied by ten times the total number of milliliters.
 CRITICAL STEP $3\times10^{6}$ cells are needed for cell differentiation.
(v) Centrifuge $3\times10^{6}$ hiPSCs for 3 min at $270g$ at room temperature.
 CRITICAL STEP For cell maintenance, retain a further $3\substack{-6\times10^{5}}$ hiPSCs and replate them in a T25 flask.
(vi) Aspirate the supernatant and resuspend the cells you are differentiating in Essential 8 medium with $10\upmu\mathsf{M}$ ROCK inhibitor (Y27632) at a concentration of $6\times10^{5}$ hiPSCs per ml and plate $2.5~\mathsf{m l}$ per well of a six-well plate.
 CRITICAL STEP $100\%$ confluence is critical to efficient CM differentiation. Therefore, after adding the cells, tap the plate from all sides and leave it inside the hood at room temperature for ${\sim}15$ min. This will enable more homogeneous plating of the cells.
(vii) To initiate cell differentiation the next day, change the medium to RPMI plus B27 minus insulin and $6~\upmu\upmu$ CHIR99021 and incubate in the $5\%$ ${\mathsf{C}}0_{2}$ incubator for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ .
$\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL STEP To maintain consistency and increase differentiation efficiency, all incubation times stated in this PROCEDURE should remain unchanged. In addition, the medium should always be added slowly to avoid mechanical stress, which can affect cell differentiation. Use $2.5~\mathsf{m l}$ of medium per well.
(viii) Change the medium to RPMI plus B27 minus insulin and incubate in the $5\%$ ${\mathsf{C}}0_{2}$ incubator for $24\ h$ . (ix) Change the medium to RPMI plus B27 minus insulin and $10\upmu\upmu$ XAV939, and incubate in the $5\%$ ${\mathsf{C}}0_{2}$ incubator for  CRITICAL STEP If you wish to continue to grow cells in culture rather than proceed to transplantation, change the medium of the cells to RPMI plus B27 minus insulin and incubate in the $5\%$ ${\mathsf{C}}0_{2}$ incubator for a further $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then change the medium to RPMI plus B27 without vitamin A and incubate in the $5\%$ ${\mathsf{C}}0_{2}$ incubator for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . At this stage, most cells will be contracting CMs. Cells can be cultured for 1 month, during which time continue changing the medium to RPMI plus B27 without vitamin A every other day. Cells can then be analyzed as described in Box 2.
